#!/usr/bin/env bash
# PreToolUse on Bash: block a state or clock change driven straight
# through emacsclient.
#
# TODO.org :ID: 3cd7b7d3. The rule was "if the emacs-tools server is not
# connected, stop and say so rather than reaching for emacsclient": a
# direct `org-todo' applies at once and writes NOTHING to the queue, so
# the change is invisible to the review pass and to org_pending_updates --
# and for any keyword that logs, the call hangs on `*Org Note*'. On
# 2026-08-15 three state changes went that way and nothing in the
# transcript looked wrong. The fallback is tempting exactly when the tools
# are down, which is when a sentence is least likely to be recalled.
#
# Narrow: only a command that invokes emacsclient AND calls one of the
# three commands as a function -- an opening paren directly before the
# name, a delimiter directly after. Reading state, the documented
# `claude-code-ide-org-close-open-interval' recovery call, and grepping
# source for the string are all untouched.
#
# Blocks with exit 2 and stderr, the documented PreToolUse path, and
# counts itself as a miss. Plain bash + jq; fails open.

set -uo pipefail

command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1 || exit 0
input="$(cat)"
cmd="$(jq -r '.tool_input.command // empty' <<<"$input" 2>/dev/null)"
[[ -n "$cmd" ]] || exit 0

grep -q 'emacsclient' <<<"$cmd" || exit 0
grep -qE '\((org-todo|org-clock-in|org-clock-out)([[:space:])"'"'"']|$)' <<<"$cmd" || exit 0

jq -c '{session_id, cwd, hook_event_name}' <<<"$input" 2>/dev/null \
  | "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")/queue-append" miss emacsclient-state "${cmd:0:200}" 2>/dev/null

cat >&2 <<'MSG'
Blocked: this drives org-todo / org-clock-in / org-clock-out directly
through emacsclient. That applies immediately and writes nothing to the
queue, so the review pass and org_pending_updates never see it -- and for
a keyword that logs, the call hangs on *Org Note*. Use org_set_todo /
org_clock_in / org_clock_out, which queue the change. If the emacs-tools
server is unreachable, stop and tell the user instead.
MSG
exit 2
